During the public portions of public meetings and public hearings, members of the Monticello Council shall not:
(1) Speak without first being recognized by the mayor;
(2) Interrupt anyone who has the floor;
(3) Use profanity during the public portion of any meeting;
(4) Refuse to yield the floor, or argue with the mayor, if he is ruled out of order by the mayor;
(5) Make disparaging remarks about any person's character during the public portion of any meeting. By way of example, it would be permissible to say "I cannot accept that," but it is improper to say "He's a liar," or "He can't be trusted." Remarks should be limited to the subject under discussion, and shall not address the character of the persons involved. Nothing herein shall limit or restrict the free and open debate of these matters in executive or closed session.
(6) Confront anyone about a prior statement in an accusatory manner during the public portion of any meeting. By way of example, it would be permissible to ask someone if something different had been said on a prior occasion, and the question should be phrased "Did you tell me ______________?" or "Do you remember telling me __________?"" It is impermissible to say "Didn't you tell me ____________," or "Why did you tell me ___________ before the meeting?" The purpose of the public portion of a council meeting is to allow each council member, city representative and members of the public to state their positions in an orderly fashion; it is not a trial where persons are to be cross-examined, interrogated, belittled or held out for public ridicule or embarrassment. Nothing herein shall limit or restrict the free and open debate of these matters in executive session.
(7) State during the public portion of any meeting that a city employee has not done his or her job, or has not done his or her job properly. By way of example, it would be permissible to ask if a city employee did or did not do a specific task, but the question must be phased in such a way that a simple "yes" or "no" answer is all that is requested. It would also be permissible to state or comment that a job was not done, or that it was not done correctly, so long as the person responsible is not singled out for blame; remarks concerning the subject of job performance are proper, but negative references to the person responsible are not. It is not proper to accuse city employees of not doing their jobs, or doing them improperly, during the public portions of the meeting. City employees shall not be placed in the position of responding to accusations of wrong-doing by a council member during the public portions of a meeting (unless the meeting has been called as a hearing for the express purpose of determining disciplinary action), nor shall their integrity or competency be challenged by a council member during the public portion of any meeting. Nothing herein shall limit or restrict the free and open debate of these matters in executive session.
(8) Represent their personal position or beliefs as being the positions or beliefs of the mayor and council as a whole. By way of example, unless and until a majority vote has been obtained on a specific issue, a council member shall not state that the council will or will not do something, nor shall a council member state what the board's vote will be on an issue. No single council member can speak on behalf of the council unless specifically authorized by majority vote to do so.
(9) Refuse to recognize the validity of action approved by majority vote of the council. By way of example, a council member is free to state that he does or does not agree with the majority vote, or that he would like to see the majority reconsider its action and vote a different way. However, a council member shall not refuse to sign any ordinance, resolution, contract or other legal document because he did not vote for the action taken. The minutes shall reflect his opposition to the majority's decision, but he shall not be authorized to withhold his signature on the basis that he did not vote for the action taken.
(10) Divulge the subject matter of any confidential information, legal advice, or strategy discussions revealed during an executive session. There may be occasions when a commissioner will be asked to make a specific commitment to this rule in connection with a particular case. If a commissioner is unwilling to pledge his commitment to the other members of the mayor and council, he shall be excluded from participating in that portion of the executive session.
(Ord. No. 08-07-08-1, § I, 7-8-08)

115:
The mayor and council shall have the responsibility to police itself and to enforce this article. Any council member shall have the right to bring a suspected violation of the ordinance to the attention of the full council. Any such accusation may be deliberated upon in executive session, but a vote upon any penalty to be imposed must be taken in open, public session, with all members of the mayor and council present. If the council determines by majority vote that a violation of this article has occurred, the council shall have the authority to impose one or more of the following penalties upon the offending council member:
(1) A verbal censure that shall be noted in the minutes without further elaboration;
(2) A written censure, to be made a part of the minutes, specifically outlining the facts underlying the violation;
(3) Removal of the council member from membership on any committee, association or group in which the offending council member is in involved in his capacity as a member of the mayor and council; removal shall take place by the delivery of a letter, signed by the mayor and council, to the head of such committee, association, or group, stating that the offending council member is no longer authorized to represent Monticello on said committee. This sanction shall last for a period of up to one year from the date of offence;
(4) A fine not to exceed $500.00 per offense, which shall be paid by the offending council member to the general fund of Monticello, Georgia within ten days of the mayor and council's vote establishing a violation; if the offending council member fails to make this payment within the time specified herein, the amount of the penalty shall be withheld from the offending council member's compensation as a council member.
The decision of the Monticello Mayor and Council on any question concerning a violation of this article, or the penalty imposed, shall be final, it being intent of all the council member to govern themselves, and not to abdicate this responsibility to any other person or entity.
(Ord. No. 08-07-08-1, § III, 7-8-08)

